card.statement:
Fix a vector
$v=(v_{ij})_{i\leq j}^n\in \mathbb{C}^{\frac{n(n+1)}{2}}$.

- [(i)] By $T(v)$ we will denote the following array with complex entries $\{v_{ij}\}$
\begin{center}

\Stone{ {$v_{n1}$}}\Stone{ {$v_{n2}$}} $\cdots$ \Stone{ {$v_{n,n-1}$}}\Stone{ {$v_{nn}$}}
[0.2pt]
\Stone{ {$v_{n-1,1}$}} $\cdots$ \Stone{ {$v_{n-1,n-1}$}}
[0.3cm]
$\cdots$ $\cdots$ $\cdots$
[0.3cm]
\Stone{ {$v_{21}$}}\Stone{ {$v_{22}$}}
[0.2pt]
\Stone{ {$v_{11}$}}

\end{center}
Such an array will be called a Gelfand-Tsetlin tableau of height $n$.

- [(ii)] Throughout the paper, for any ring $R$, $T_n(R)$ will stand for the space of the Gelfand-Tsetlin
tableaux of height $n$ with entries in $R$. We will identify $ T_{n}(\mathbb{C})$ with the set $\mathbb{C}^{\frac{n(n+1)}{2}}$ in the following way:
to

$$
v=(v_{n1},...,v_{nn}|v_{n-1,1},...,v_{n-1,n-1}| \cdots|v_{21}, v_{22}|v_{11})\in  \mathbb{C}^{\frac{n(n+1)}{2}}
$$

we associate a tableau $T(v)\in T_{n}(\mathbb{C})$ as above.
